WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.960
Break down how that inheritance actually

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:03.260
looks in practice for us.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.960
Sure. In the Lakeshore Retail example

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.760
provided in the source, they start

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.739
with a very broad base type

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.159
called location. A location simply

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.280
has properties like an address, geographic

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.979
coordinates, and a manager name.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.260
You can then create a store

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.500
entity that derives from location.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.920
So it inherits all that base

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.540
stuff. The store automatically inherits

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.560
the address and coordinates, but then

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:29.300
you add store specific properties,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.860
like retail square footage.

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:32.800
Similarly, you could have a base

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.139
entity called product and derive a

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:37.160
highly specific entity called frozen

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:37.659
product.
